WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
YUFENG GUO: In this episode
of Cloud AI Adventurers,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.320
find out how to use TensorBoard
to visualize your model

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.830
and debug problems.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.630
Debugging problems
is a lot easier

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.710
when you can see
what the problem is.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.150
But with gigs of training data
feeding into a complex model,

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.130
that can get complicated.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.010
Thankfully, TensorBoard
makes this a lot easier.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.170
Unlike traditional
programming, machine learning

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.730
can be very unpredictable.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.430
The quality of your data, along
with the nuances of your model,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.890
including the many parameters
that must be chosen,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.070
all have wide ranging influences
on the success or failure

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.870
of the training process.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.900
If only there was some way to
track some of these metrics

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.790
through the training
process, and also

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.190
look at the structure of
the model that we created,

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.070
that would enable us to
tune the model and debug

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.020
problems that we see.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.170
Now, this abstract process
can be difficult to visualize.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.650
But luckily, TensorFlow
has a built in solution.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.290
Meet TensorBoard,
TensorFlow's built

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.470
in visualizer, which enables you
to do a wide range of things,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.800
from seeing your model structure
to watching your training

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.190
progress.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.810
TensorFlow uses the idea
of computational graphs

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.040
under the hood.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.780
This means that
instead of adding

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.820
two numbers in the
traditional sense,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.450
it constructs an add operator
and passes in as inputs

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.530
the values being added together.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.980
So when we think about
TensorFlow training your model,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.810
it's really executing
everything as part of its graph.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.370
TensorBoard can
visualize these models,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.289
so you can see what they look
like and, more importantly,

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.830
ensure that you've wired all
the pieces of the way you wanted

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.670
to.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.680
Here's an example of
a more complex version

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.770
of a model graph being
visualized by TensorFlow.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.820
Notice how TensorFlow allows
us to zoom, pan, and expand

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.514
elements to see more details.

00:01:43.514 --> 00:01:45.180
This means that we
can look at the model

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.670
at different layers of
abstraction, which can help

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.730
reduce the visual complexity.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.800
Moreover, TensorBoard
does more than just

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.240
show the model structure.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.480
It can also plot the progression
of metrics on a nice graph.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.930
Typically, we might plot things
like accuracy, loss, cross

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:01.755
entropy.

00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:03.630
And depending on the
model, different metrics

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.150
can be important for you.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.720
And TensorFlow's
Candice emitters,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.460
they come with a set of
values pre-configured

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.100
to be shown in TensorBoard.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.350
So that serves as a
great starting point.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.720
TensorBoard can display
also a wide variety

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.750
of other information, including
histograms, distributions,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.690
embeddings, as well as
the audio, pictures,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.580
and text data that
goes with your model.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.800
But that part's
for a future video.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.590
For now, let's take
a look at an example

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.840
of TensorBoard with
the linear model

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.250
that we've been using so far.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.060
First, we'll start TensorBoard
and point it to the directory

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.660
where our model structure and
checkpoint files are saved.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.380
This will start a local
server on point port 6006.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.081
And yes, that spells out GOOG.

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:50.340
We can see here some
of the scalar metrics

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.070
that are provided by default
with the linear classifier.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.400
We can also expand and zoom
into any of these graphs.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.190
Double clicking allows
us to zoom back out.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.200
You can see our training
progressed nicely

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.900
with a decreasing
loss over time.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.180
It's also clear that the
training hasn't entirely

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.150
completed, as the loss is still
decreasing at a nice rate,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.500
even at the end of training.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.390
So this might clue
us in that perhaps we

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.400
could run the training
process a bit longer,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.970
and get the most out of
this training process.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.280
Let's go over to graphs tab now.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.470
Notice that on the surface,
the graph is pretty simple.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.320
We're able to expand
each of these blocks

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.000
by clicking the plus
sign to see more detail.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.660
For example, if I
expand the linear block,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.970
we see that it's made up of
a number of sub-components.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.520
And we can scroll
to zoom in and out,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.950
as well as click and
drag to pan and see

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.820
other parts of the graph.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.310
Notice also that
the names we gave

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.560
our future columns,
like flower features,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.110
show up as named
graph components.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.420
This can help with
debugging and identifying

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.550
how the graph is all hooked up.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.890
Most of TensorFlow's
operations can be named.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.020
So that's a great way
to clarify your models

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.940
and see what's going on.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.590
Today, we learned
that machine learning

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.650
is much easier and more fun
when you can visualize the model

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.470
along with important metrics.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.400
TensorBoard allows you
to do this with ease,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.340
and better yet, it's
built into TensorFlow.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:09.420
I'm Yufeng Guo.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.330
And thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.970
And remember, next time you
need to visualize your machine

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.950
learning, use
TensorBoard to see what's

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.092
happening under the hood.

